\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Let $X$ be a hyperkähler manifold of complex dimension $2n$. Is its algebraic dimension necessarily in $\{0,n,2n\}$? If $a(X)=n$, does the algebraic reduction admit a holomorphic model $f:X\to B$, where $B$ is a normal projective variety of dimension $n$, and hence define a Lagrangian fibration? Does parabolic $\operatorname{NS}(X)$ imply $a(X)=n$?
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2008/38}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Let $X$ be a hyperkähler manifold of complex dimension $2n$. Is its algebraic dimension necessarily in $\{0,n,2n\}$? If $a(X)=n$, does the algebraic reduction admit a holomorphic model $f:X\to B$, where $B$ is a normal projective variety of dimension $n$, and hence define a Lagrangian fibration? Does parabolic $\operatorname{NS}(X)$ imply $a(X)=n$?

